\rem source G. R. S. Mead, A Mithriac Ritual (Echoes from the Gnosis, vol. VI, London and Benares: Theosophical Publishing Society, 1907), pp. 18-33, "The Ritual" only (his preamble and comments left out); transcribed from the archive.org scan, item amythicritu06meaduoft, its OCR corrected against the page images. Mead translates Dieterich's text of the Paris Magical Papyrus (PGM IV 475-829); his italics (the prayers, as against the rubrics) are not kept.
\rem rights Public domain (published 1907)
\h The Mithras Liturgy
\toc1 The Mithras Liturgy
\mt1 The Mithras Liturgy
\c 1
\p
\v 1 \vp I\vp* O Providence, O Fortune, bestow on me Thy Grace—imparting these the Mysteries a Father only may hand on, and that, too, to a Son alone—his Immortality—[a Son] initiate, worthy of this our Craft, with which Sun Mithras, the Great God, commanded me to be endowed by His Archangel; so that I, Eagle [as I am, by mine own self] alone, may soar to Heaven, and contemplate all things.
\v 2 \vp II\vp* 1. O Primal Origin of my origination; Thou Primal Substance of my substance; First Breath of breath, the breath that is in me; First Fire, God-given for the Blending of the blendings in me, [First Fire] of fire in me; First Water of [my] water, the water in me; Primal Earth-essence of the earthy essence in me; Thou Perfect Body of me—N. N., son of N. N. (fem.)—fashioned by Honoured Arm and Incorruptible Right Hand, in World that’s lightless, yet radiant with Light, [in World] that’s soulless, yet filled full of Soul!
\p 2. If, verily, it may seem good to you, translate me, now held by my lower nature, unto the Generation that is free from Death; in order that, beyond the insistent Need that presses on me, I may have Vision of the Deathless Source, by virtue of the Deathless Spirit, by virtue of the Deathless Water, by virtue of the [Deathless] Solid, and [by virtue of] the [Deathless] Air; in order that I may become re-born in Mind; in order that I may become initiate, and that the Holy Breath may breathe in me; in order that I may admire the Holy Fire; that I may see the Deep of the [New] Dawn, the Water that doth cause [the Soul] to thrill; and that the Life-bestowing Æther which surrounds [all things] may give me Hearing.
\p 3. For I am to behold to-day with Deathless Eyes—I, mortal, born of mortal womb, but [now] made better by the Might of Mighty Power, yea, by the Incorruptible Right Hand—[I am to see to-day] by virtue of the Deathless Spirit the Deathless Æon, the Master of the Diadems of Fire—I with pure purities [now] purified, the human soul-power of me subsisting for a little while in purity; which [power] I shall again receive transmitted unto me beyond the insistent Bitterness that presses on me, Necessity whose debts can never go unpaid—I, N. N., son of N. N. (fem.)—according to the Ordinance of God that naught can ever change.
\p 4. For that it is beyond my reach that, born beneath the sway of Death, I should [unaided] soar into the Height, together with the golden sparklings of the Brilliancy that knows no Death.
\p 5. Stay still, O nature doomed to perish, [nature] of men subject to Death! And straightway let me pass beyond the Need implacable that presses on me; for that I am His Son; I breathe; I am!
\v 3 \vp III\vp* 1. Take from the [Sun-]rays breath, inhaling thrice [as deeply] as thou canst; and thou shalt see thyself being raised aloft, and soaring towards the Height, so that thou seem’st to be in midst of Air.
\p 2. Thou shalt hear naught, nor man nor beast; nor shalt thou see aught of the sights upon the earth, in that same hour; but all things thou shalt see will be immortal.
\p 3. For thou shalt see, in that same day and hour, the Disposition of the Gods—the Ruling Gods ascending heavenwards, the other ones descending.
\p And through his Disk—the God’s, my Father’s—there shall be seen the Way-of-going of the Gods accessible to sight.
\p 4. And in like fashion also [shall be seen] the Pipe, as it is called, whence comes the Wind in service [for the day].
\p For thou shalt see as though it were a Pipe depending from His Disk; and toward the regions Westward, as though it were an infinite East Wind. But if the other Wind, toward the regions of the East, should be in service, in the like fashion shalt thou see, toward the regions of that [side,] the converse of the sight.
\p 5. And thou shalt see the Gods gazing intently on thee and bearing down upon thee.
\p Then straightway lay thy dexter finger on thy lips and say:
\v 4 \vp IV\vp* O Silence! Silence! Silence!
\p The Symbol of the Living God beyond Decay.
\p Protect me, Silence! †!
\p Next “hiss” forth long: S̄! S̄!
\p Then “puff” saying: †!
\p And thereon shalt thou see the Gods gazing benignly on thee, and no longer bearing down upon thee, but proceeding on the proper order of their doings.
\v 5 \vp V\vp* When, then, thou see’st the Upper Cosmos clean and clear, with no one of the Gods (or Angels) bearing down on thee, expect to hear a mighty thunder-clap so as to startle thee.
\p Then say again:
\v 6 \vp VI\vp* 1. O Silence! Silence!
\q1 I am a Star, whose Course is as your Course, shining anew from out the Depth †.
\p Upon thy saying this, straightway His Disk will start expanding.
\p 2. And after thou hast said the second utterance—to wit, twice Silence and the rest—“hiss” twice, and “puff” twice; and straightway shalt thou see a mighty host of stars, five-pointed, emerging from His Disk, and filling all the Air.
\p 3. Then say again:
\p O Silence! Silence!
\p And when His Disk is opened [fully] out, thou shalt behold an infinite Encircling and Doors of Fire fast closed.
\p Straightway set going then the utterance that follows, closing thy eyes:
\v 7 \vp VII\vp* 1. Hear me, give ear to me—N. N., son of N. N. (fem.)—O Lord, who with Thy Breath hast closed the Fiery Bars of Heaven; Twin-bodied; Ruler of the Fire; Creator of the Light; O Holder of the Keys; Inbreather of the Fire; Fire-hearted One, whose Breath gives Light; Thou who dost joy in Fire; Beauteous of Light; O Lord of Light, whose Body is of Fire; Light-giver [and] Fire-sower; Fire-loosener, whose Life is in the Light; Fire-whirler, who sett’st the Light in motion; Thou Thunder-rouser; O Thou Light-glory, Light-increaser; Controller of the Light Empyrean; O Thou Star-tamer!
\p 2. Oh! Open unto me! For on account of this, the bitter and implacable Necessity that presses on me, I do invoke Thy Deathless Names, innate with Life, most worshipful, that have not yet descended unto mortal nature, nor have been made articulate by human tongue, or cry or tone of man:
\p Ēeō · oēeō · iōō · oē · ēeō · ēeō · oēeō · iōō · oēēe · ōēe · ōoē · iē · ēō · oō · oē · ieō · oē · ōoē · ieōoē · ieeō · eē · iō · oē · ioē · ōēō · eoē · oeō · ōiē · ōiēeō · oi · iii · ēoē · ōuē · ēō · oēe · eōēia · aēaeēa · ēeeē · eeē · eeē · ieō · ēeō · oēeeoē · ēeō · euō · oē · eiō · ēō · ōē · ōē · ōē · ee · ooouiōē!
\p 3. Utter all these with Fire and Spirit once unto the end; and then begin again a second time, until thou hast completed [all] the Seven Immortal Gods of Cosmos.
\p When thou hast uttered them, thunders and crashings shalt thou hear in the Surround, and feel thyself a-shake with every crash.
\p Then once more utter Silence! [and] the utterance [following it].
\p 4. Thereon open thy eyes; and thou shalt see the Doors thrown open, and the Cosmos of the Gods that is within the Doors; so that for joy and rapture of the sight thy Spirit runs to meet it, and soars up.
\p Therefore, hold thyself steady, and, gazing steadily into thyself, draw breath from the Divine.
\p When, then, thy Soul shall be restored, say:
\v 8 \vp VIII\vp* 1. Draw nigh, O Lord!
\p Upon this utterance His Rays shall be turned on thee, and thou shalt be in midst of them.
\p 2. When, then, thou hast done this, thou shalt behold a God, in flower of age, of fairest beauty, [and] with Locks of Flame, in a white Tunic and a scarlet Mantle, wearing a Crown of Fire.
\p Straightway salute Him with the Salutation of the Fire:
\v 9 \vp IX\vp* 1. Hail Lord! O Thou of mighty Power; O King of mighty Sway; Greatest of Gods; O Sun; Thou Lord of Heaven and Earth; O God of Gods! Strong is Thy Breath; strong is Thy Might!
\p O Lord, if it seem good to Thee, make Thou announcement of me unto God Most-high, who hath begotten and created Thee!
\p 2. For that a man—I, N.N., son of N.N. (fem.), born of the mortal womb of N.N. (fem.), and of spermatic ichōr, yea, of this [ichōr], which at Thy Hands to-day hath undergone the transmutation of re-birth—, one, from so many tens of thousands, transformed to immortality in this same hour, by God’s good-pleasure, of God transcendent Good—, [a man, I say,] presumes to worship Thee, and supplicates with whatsoever power a mortal hath.
\p 3. Upon this utterance He shall come to the Pole, and thou shalt see Him moving round as on a path.
\p Then gaze intently, and send forth a prolonged “bellowing,” like to a horn-note, expelling the whole breath, with pressure on the ribs, and kiss the amulets, and say first to that upon the right:
\v 10 \vp X\vp* Protect me! †!
\p When thou hast uttered this, thou shalt behold the Doors thrown open, and, issuing from the Depth, Seven Virgins, in byssus-robes, with serpent-faces, and golden sceptres in their hands. These are they who are the so-called Heaven’s Fortunes (Tychai).
\p When thou dost see these things, make salutation thus:
\v 11 \vp XI\vp* 1. Hail Heaven’s Seven Fortunes, Virgins august and good, ye sacred ones who live and eat with †! Ye holiest Protectors of the Four Supports!
\p Hail thou, the First, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Second, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Third, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Fourth, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Fifth, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Sixth, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Seventh, †!
\p 2. There come forth others, too—Seven Gods, with faces of black bulls, in linen loin-cloths, with seven golden fillets on their heads. These are the so-called Heaven’s Pole-lords.
\p And in like fashion unto each of them thou must make salutation with his special name.
\v 12 \vp XII\vp* 1. Hail Guardians of the Pivot, ye sacred sturdy Youths, who all, at once, revolve the spinning Axis of Heaven’s Circle, ye who let loose the thunder and the lightning, and earthquake-shocks and thunder-bolts upon the hosts of impious folk, but [who bestow] on me, who pious am and worshipper of God, good-health, and soundness of my frame in every part, and proper stretch of hearing and of sight, and calm, in the now present good-hours of this day, O mighty Ruling Lords and Gods of me!
\p Hail thou, the First, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Second, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Third, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Fourth, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Fifth, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Sixth, †!
\q1 Hail thou, the Seventh. †!
\p 2. Now when they [all] are present in their order, here and there, gaze in the Air intently, and thou shalt see lightnings down-flashing, and lights a-quiver, and the earth a-shake; and [then] a God descending, [a God] transcending vast, of radiant Presence, with golden Locks, in flower of age, [clad] in a Robe of brightness, with Crown of gold [upon His Head], and Garments [on His Legs], holding in His Right Hand the golden Shoulder of the Calf.
\p This latter is the Bear that moves the Heaven[-dome], and changes its direction, now up now down, according to the hour.
\p Then shalt thou see lightnings leap from His Eyes and from His Body stars.
\p 3. Straightway send forth a “bellowing” prolonged, with belly-pressure, to start thy senses going all together—prolonged unto the very end, kissing again the amulets and saying:
\v 13 \vp XIII\vp* †, [O Lord] of me—N. N.—abide with me, within my Soul! Oh! leave me not! For † bids thee [remain].
\p And gaze intently on the God, with “bellowing” prolonged, and thus salute Him:
\v 14 \vp XIV\vp* Hail Lord, Thou Master of the Water! Hail, Founder of the Earth! Hail, Prince of Breath!
\p O Lord, being born again, I pass away in being made Great, and, having been made Great, I die.
\p Being born from out the state of birth-and-death that giveth birth to [mortal] lives, I now, set free, pass to the state transcending birth, as Thou hast stablished it, according as Thou hast ordained and made the Mystery.
